      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 504,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in oceans, rivers, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity in the air)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and unusual properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans stay salty.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n* **Expansion When Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands and becomes *less* dense when it freezes. This is why ice floats on water, allowing marine life to survive under frozen lakes during the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Blueprint of Life:** All known forms of life depend on water. The human body is made up of about 60% water, and cells require a watery environment to carry out their chemical reactions.\n* **Earth's Lifeblood:** Often called the \"Blue Planet,\" about 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that acts as the absolute foundation for chemistry, climate, and life as we know it."
